          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following cell types arises from the common lymphoid progenitor (CL</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following cell types arises from the common lymphoid progenitor (CLP) rather than the common myeloid progenitor (CMP)?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Erythrocyte</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Erythrocytes arise from the CMP lineage via erythropoiesis, driven by erythropoietin produced by the kidney.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neutrophil</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Neutrophils belong to the myeloid lineage (CMP), stimulated by granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Platelet</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Platelets arise from megakaryocytes in the CMP lineage, driven by thrombopoietin from the liver and kidney.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Natural killer cell</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Natural killer cells, along with T and B lymphocytes, arise from the common lymphoid progenitor (CLP).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The HSC divides into two great lineages: the CMP (red cells, platelets, granulocytes, monocytes) and the CLP (T cells, B cells, NK cells). Review which cells belong to each branch."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! The CLP gives rise to T cells, B cells, and NK cells. All other blood cells — red cells, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes — descend from the CMP."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
