          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A peripheral blood smear shows nucleated red blood cells, immature granulocytes,</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A peripheral blood smear shows nucleated red blood cells, immature granulocytes, and teardrop-shaped cells. What is the most appropriate next investigation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ferritin and iron studies</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Iron studies assess iron deficiency anaemia, which presents with microcytic hypochromic cells — not the leukoerythroblastic triad described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow trephine biopsy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The leukoerythroblastic triad — nucleated RBCs, immature granulocytes, and teardrop cells — signals extramedullary haematopoiesis from marrow pathology and mandates a biopsy to identify the cause.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemoglobin electrophoresis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemoglobin electrophoresis detects haemoglobinopathies. While beta-thalassaemia can cause EMH, the immediate step for a leukoerythroblastic smear is marrow biopsy to establish the underlying pathology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Direct Coombs test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The direct Coombs test detects antibody-coated red cells in autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, which does not produce this characteristic triad.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When nucleated RBCs, immature granulocytes, and teardrop cells appear together on a peripheral smear, this leukoerythroblastic picture indicates marrow pathology. A bone marrow trephine biopsy is the mandatory next step."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The leukoerythroblastic triad is the hallmark of marrow infiltration or failure driving extramedullary haematopoiesis. Bone marrow biopsy is essential to establish the underlying cause."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
