          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>After staining a peripheral blood smear, in which zone should you position the m</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>After staining a peripheral blood smear, in which zone should you position the microscope field to assess red and white blood cell morphology?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The thick head near the frosted end</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The head zone has densely packed, overlapping cells that cannot be individually assessed for morphology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The feathered tail at the far end</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The feathered tail has excessively spread and distorted cells. While large abnormal cells may be spotted here, routine morphology is not assessed in this zone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The body in the middle of the smear</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The body zone has evenly spaced cells, each showing a clear central pallor — the ideal area for reliable morphological assessment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Any zone, as cell distribution is uniform across the smear</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cell distribution varies markedly across the three zones. Only the body zone provides the even spacing needed for accurate morphological evaluation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A peripheral blood smear has three zones: the thick head (cells too crowded), the body (evenly spaced — morphology read here), and the feathered tail (cells too spread). Always examine the body zone."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! A well-made smear has three zones — head, body, and tail. The body is where cells are optimally spaced for morphology, making it the standard examination area."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
